## 7. Visual Direction & Graphics Scope

### 7.1 Style Statement
Vibrant, expressive, exaggerated and captivating. It is the key-art style of Pixiv and Danbooru illustration, with ornate temple-craft detailing, rendered as **PS2/PSP-era 3D with a high-budget Japanese AAA finish**. Beauty comes from stylization, composition and color design, not from polygon counts.

### 7.2 Graphics Implementation Scope

| Element | Scope |
|---|---|
| **Characters** | 7–10k triangles each (armored marionettes at the upper end); hand-painted 512px textures; two-tone cel shading with a painted shadow layer and thin inked outlines (variable weight); strong rim light |
| **Faces** | Texture-swap expressions (eyes, brows, mouth), as on PS2 games; large Danbooru-style eyes with highlight shapes. Armored fighters have fixed painted faceplates that change only through light and cracks |
| **Hair and cloth** | Simple bone-chain secondary motion for ponytails, twintails, skirts, tassels, ribbon-scarves, strings and hoses |
| **Halos and extra arms** | Halos are billboarded geometry discs with scrolling emissive textures. Spectral arms are translucent skinned meshes sharing the character skeleton with delayed playback |
| **Stages** | 40–70k triangles; baked lighting; hand-painted textures; painted sky domes; alpha-card fog layers, light shafts and fake volumetrics |
| **Shadows** | Projected planar shadows and blob shadows |
| **Lighting** | One key light plus a rim light per fighter, color-scripted per Tempo phase |
| **Water** | Mirrored geometry and a painted ripple overlay |
| **FX** | flipbook sprites (ink brush slashes, blood ribbons, lotus petals, sparks, incense smoke) billboarded in 3D |
| **Animation** | Stepped, limited-key animation with smear frames |
| **Impact frames** | Two-frame inverted ink flashes |
| **Frame rate** | Locked 60 fps at 720p, scaling up |

### 7.4 Camera
- A 2.5D side-on camera with a slightly low angle and a mild telephoto feel.
- It zooms for impacts and swings in 3D for Rites and stage transitions.
- Faux depth-of-field comes from painted blurred foreground cards (reeds, railings, wind-bell tassels).

### 7.5 Gore and Violence Presentation
- Stylized but explicit: thick painterly blood ribbons, arterial sprays, wound decals, and dismemberment silhouettes on Rite finishers.
- Marionette fighters bleed splinters, porcelain shards and black silk instead of blood.
- Gore can be toggled down in options.

